   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to legalize, validate, ratify and confirm the actions of the\nSpackenkill union free school district notwithstanding the failure to timely\nfile final building cost reports with the education department",
    "basePrintNo": "S8603",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8603-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Education",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8603\n\nSPONSOR: SERINO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to legalize, validate, ratify and confirm the\nactions of the Spackenkill union free school district notwithstanding\nthe failure to timely file final building cost reports with the educa-\ntion department\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo forgive a penalty levied on the Spackenkill Union Free School\nDistrict for filling a late final cost report.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Legalizes, validates, ratifies and confirms the actions of\nthe Spackenkill Union Free School District regardless of the failure of\nsuch district to timely file final building cost reports that were\nrequired to be filed with the Education Department before December 31,\n2012.\n\nSection 2: Provides that the Commissioner of the State Education Depart-\nment shall not recover from the Spackenkill Union Free School District\nany penalty arising from the late filing of a final cost report pursuant\nto section 31 of part A of chapter 57 of the laws of 2012, provided that\nany amounts already so recovered shall be deemed a payment for money due\nfor prior years pursuant to paragraph c of subdivision 5 of section 3604\nof the education law and shall be paid to the Spackenkill Union Free\nSchool District pursuant to such provision.\n\nSection 3: Provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nSpackenkill Union Free School District, a NYSED nominated Blue Ribbon\nSchool, was assessed a penalty of $9,603,719 due to filing a final\nbuilding cost report after the deadline at the end of 2012 despite the\nfact that their EXCEL grant money was not disbursed until March of 2015.\nThe penalty levied on the school district is nearly equal to the state\naid received by the district, and is excessive given the modest size of\nthe school.\n\nThe Spackenkill Union Free School District voters approved a $13,934,000\nproject for renovations and an addition to Spackenkill High School in\n2009. The architectural firm enlisted for the project filed Certificates\nof Substantial Completion forms with Facilities Planning in 2010 and\n2011. In February 2015 the district's business manager, requested in\nwriting an extension to file the Final Cost Report (FCR) because the\ndistrict could not close out the project because the EXCEL money from\nthe Dormitory Authority had not been received, and the district was\nnegotiating with three vendors over billing and work performance.\n\nOne month later in March 2015, the Business Manager received correspond-\nence that the EXCEL payment would be made to the district.  In June\n2016, the school's former Superintendent signed the Final Cost Report\nand it was submitted to Facilities Planning.\n\nIn February 2018, the current Superintendent and Business Manager were\nmade aware of the building aid penalty. The superintendent went to the\nFacilities Planning web site upon learning of this issue and could find\nlittle to no information on the project or any indication of lateness.\nThe superintendent contacted the State Department of Education and\nlearned of the penalty. The district was told that this penalty would be\namortized over several years and have estimated that the annual penalty\nwould be almost $1,000,000 per year on the $46,000,000 school budget.\n\nThe district is in receipt of correspondence indicating that the FCR was\ndue by June 30, 2013. This date does not acknowledge that the project\ncould not be closed out until EXCEL money was received in 2015. No other\ncorrespondence or warning was given to the district, a point exasperated\nby the fact that the district chose the expedited review process for the\ncurrent capital project that was passed in December 2017. At no time\nduring this planning and approval process for this project did state\nofficials indicate that the FCR was late from a prior project. The\ndistrict paid the additional money for an expedited review in January\n2018, but was told that the check was never received. The district later\nlearned that the check was applied to another district's filing.\n\nWhile the 2018-19 state budget included critically important provisions\nthat will reduce the district's penalty modestly, the district will\nstill be required to pay back millions. This legislation would forgive\nthe remaining amount of the penalty to help preserve the high quality\neducational programs that the district has worked to create and main-\ntain.\n\nThe district understands the need for accountability and established\ndeadlines in this process, however to levy such a severe penalty against\na district for what amounts to no more than a highly atypical adminis-\ntrative error, will do a significant disservice to the community's\ntaxpayers, and most importantly its students.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTBD.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
